cle 23.2.3.\n\nFORMULA\n\na(n) = A244040(n) mod 2.\n\na(3n) = a(2n), a(3n+1) = 1-a(2n), a(3n+2) = a(2n).\n\nEXAMPLE\n\n010010 -> 010010101-> 010010101101-> ....\n\nCROSSREFS\n\nCf. A354896, A244040. The base 2 version: A010060.\n\nSequence in context: A189572 A287028 A327202 * A083651 A111748 A282244\n\nAdjacent sequences: A357445 A357446 A357447 * A357449 A357450 A357451\n\nKEYWORD\n\nnonn,base\n\nAUTHOR\n\nMichel Dekking, Sep 29 2022\n\nSTATUS\n\napproved\n\n\nLast modified September 2 04:41 EDT 2026. Contains 398894 sequences.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) The Thue-Morse sequence in base 3/2 (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/367652620_The_Thue-Morse_sequence_in_base_32)\nciteturn2search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2301.13563 ... arXiv:2301.13563v1 [math.CO] 31 Jan 2023 ... Conjecture 4. x 3/2 is reversal invariant, i.e., if the word w=w 1...w m occurs in x 3/2 then ←− ... If we code further a, b 7→ 0, and c, d 7→ 1, then we obtain κ′from Theorem 10.\nPreprint PDF Available\n\n# The Thue-Morse sequence in base 3/2\n\n  * January 2023\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2301.13563\n\n  * License\n  * CC BY 4.0\n\nAuthors:\n\nMichel Dekking\n\nMichel Dekking\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage\n\nDownload file PDFRead file\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe discuss the base 3/2 representation of the natural numbers. We prove that the sum of digits function of the representation is a fixed point of a 2-block substitution on an infinite alphabet, and that this implies that sum of digits function modulo 2 of the representation is a fixed point $x_{3/2}$ of a 2-block substitution on $\\{0,1\\}$. We prove that $x_{3/2}$ is mirror invariant, and present a list of conjectured properties of $x_{3/2}$, which we think will be hard to prove. Finally, we make a comparison with a variant of the base 3/2 representation, and give a general result on p-q-block substitutions.\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nAvailable via license: CC BY 4.0\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\narXiv:2301.13563v1 [math.CO] 31 Jan 2023\n\nThe Thue-Morse sequence in base 3/2\n\nF. M. Dekking\n\nCWI and Delft University of Technology\n\nFaculty EEMCS, P.O. Box 5031\n\n2600 GA Delft, The Netherlands\n\nF.M.Dekking@math.tudelft.nl\n\nAbstract\n\nWe discuss the base 3/2 representation of the natural numbers. We prove that the sum of digits\n\nfunction of the representation is a ﬁxed point of a 2-block substitution on an inﬁnite alphabet, and that\n\nthis implies that sum of digits function modulo 2 of the representation is a ﬁxed point x 3/2 of a 2-block\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Rigidity, Tensegrity and Reconstruction of Polytopes under Metric Constraints (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/368877627_Rigidity_Tensegrity_and_Reconstruction_of_Polytopes_under_Metric_Constraints)\nciteturn2search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.7 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago; DOI:10.48550/arXiv.2302.14194 ... arXiv:2302.14194v1 [math.CO] 27 Feb 2023 ... Theorem 4.4 has a natural interpretation in the language of classical rigidity the- ... problem for d≥3(see Conjecture 5.11). ... Recall the tangent cone of P ... to the literature, as well as many questions and future research directions.\n\n# Rigidity, Tensegrity and Reconstruction of Polytopes under Metric Constraints\n\n  * February 2023\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2302.14194\n\n  * License\n  * CC BY 4.0\n\nAuthors:\n\nMartin Winter\n\nMartin Winter\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage\n\nDownload file PDFRead file\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract and Figures\n\nWe conjecture that a convex polytope is uniquely determined up to isometry by its edge-graph, edge lengths and the collection of distances of its vertices to some arbitrary interior point, across all dimensions and all combinatorial types. We conjecture even stronger that for two polytopes $P\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ and $Q\\subset\\mathbb R^e$ with the same edge-graph it is not possible that Q has longer edges than P while also having smaller vertex-point distances. We develop techniques to attack this question and verify it in three relevant special cases: if P and Q are centrally symmetric, if Q is a slight perturbation of P, and if P and Q are combinatorially equivalent. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA354896 - OEIS (https://oeis.org/A354896)\nciteturn2search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.4 years ago; Crawled: 1.4 years ago; It is an unsolved problem to show that the density of 1's is equal to 1/2. ... Michel Dekking, The Thue-Morse sequence in base 3/2, arXiv:2301.13563 [math.CO], 2023.\n\n(II) (a(n)) is uniformly recurrent, i.e., each word that occurs in (a(n)) occurs infinitely often, with bounded gaps.\n\nLINKS\n\nMichel Dekking, Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000\n\nMichel Dekking and Mike Keane, Two-block substitutions and morphic words, arXiv:2202.13548 [math.CO], 2022.\n\nMichel Dekking, The Thue-Morse sequence in base 3/2, arXiv:2301.13563 [math.CO], 2023.\n\nEXAMPLE\n\nThe first seven iterates of the mapping kappa' starting with 0011:\n\n0011\n\n001110\n\n001110101\n\n001110101101\n\n001110101101110010\n\n001110101101110010110001101\n\n001110101101110010110001101110001010101\n\nPROG\n\n(PARI) a(n) = my(ret=0, r); while(n>2, [n, r]=divrem(n, 3); n=2*n+r; ret+=!r); ret%2; \\\\ Kevin Ryde, Jun 11 2022\n\nCROSSREFS\n\nCf. A000002, A289001, A357448.\n\nSequence in context: A373141 A323158 A011657 * A359606 A072126 A287125\n\nAdjacent sequences: A354893 A354894 A354895 * A354897 A354898 A354899\n\nKEYWORD\n\nnonn\n\nAUTHOR\n\nMichel Dekking, Jun 11 2022\n\nEXTENSIONS\n\nName changed by Michel Dekking, Feb 23 2023\n\nSTATUS\n\napproved--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nASSESSING CONCEPTUAL UNDERSTANDING OF ENERGY IN (https://ocpa10-2026.sg/docs/304%20Physics%2C%20Science%20Education.pdf)\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; arXiv:2410.13575 (accepted by Communications in Mathematical Physics), H. ... Nonstabilizerness Enhances Thrifty Shadow Estimation, arXiv:2410.23977, D.\nRandomized quantum measurements in quantum information\nprocessing\nH. Zhu\nDepartment of Physics, Fudan University\nABSTRACT\nQuantum measurements are the key for extracting information from quantum systems\nand for connecting the quantum world with the classical world. For many applications\nin quantum information processing, randomized measurements have proved to be much\nmore efficient in information extraction. In this talk I will discuss the applications of\nrandomized measurements in various tasks, including verification of quantum states,\ngates, and computation, estimation of linear properties (say fidelity) and nonlinear\nproperties (say purity), certification of (high-dimensional) entanglement, and the\nlearning of unitary channels. In addition, I will explore the efficiency limits of these\ntasks and highlight the peculiar roles of quantum resources, such as entanglement and\nnonstabilizerness (magic).\nREFERENCE STYLE\n1. Qudit Shadow Estimation Based on the Clifford Group and the Power of a Single\nMagic Gate, Phys. Rev. Lett. 134, 160801, 2025, C. Mao, C. Yi, and H. Zhu.\n2. Third moments of qudit Clifford orbits and 3-designs based on magic orbits,\narXiv:2410.13575 (accepted by Communications in Mathematical Physics), H. Zhu, C.\nMao, and C. Yi.\n3. Nonstabilizerness Enhances Thrifty Shadow Estimation, arXiv:2410.23977, D.\nC